    <script type="application/ld+json">{"mainEntity":{"headline":"Integrated aquatic phytoremediation systems for multicontaminant water treatment: mechanisms, biodiversity, and industrial valorization","description":"Aquatic pollution increasingly involves complex mixtures of heavy metals (HM), organic matters (OM) such as pesticides, radionuclides, dyes, antibiotics, or industrial discharges such as landfill leachates, and tannery-derived contaminants, requiring treatment strategies capable of addressing multiple pollutant classes while minimizing environmental impacts. This review critically examines integrated aquatic phytoremediation systems by linking aquatic plant (AP) biodiversity and functional typology with contaminant-removal mechanisms, practical treatment configurations, harvested-biomass management, industrial valorization and optimization. The reviewed evidence shows that floating, emergent, and submerged APs can remove diverse contaminants through complementary processes, including phytoaccumulation, phytoextraction, rhizofiltration, phytostabilization, phytodegradation, rhizodegradation, phytotransformation, and phytovolatilization. Treatment performance is strongly governed by plant species and community composition, root–microorganism interactions, pollutant characteristics, loading conditions, pH, temperature, and hydraulic conditions. Beyond remediation, harvested aquatic biomass can support circular-economy pathways through bioenergy production, nutrient recovery, fertilizers, biomaterials, cellulose, and biochemical extraction, although contaminated biomass requires rigorous risk assessment and controlled post-harvest management. Major knowledge gaps remain in the standardization of performance metrics, long-term field validation, multicontaminant interactions, seasonal and climatic resilience, safe biomass valorization, and scale-up from laboratory studies to operational systems. Hybrid and polyculture systems offer opportunities for complementary pollutant removal but require improved understanding of interspecific competition and ecological compatibility. Future research should therefore prioritize standardized multicontaminant datasets, ecologically optimized plant assemblages, continuous sensor-based monitoring, predictive modelling, and artificial intelligence-assisted control to enable adaptive, scalable, and resource-efficient aquatic phytoremediation systems. ","datePublished":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z","dateModified":"2026-09-19T00:00:00Z","pageStart":"1","pageEnd":"46","sameAs":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s13762-026-07440-6","keywords":["Aquatic plants","Heavy metals","Organic matter","Phytoremediation","Environment","general","Environmental Science and Engineering","Environmental Chemistry","Waste Water Technology / Water Pollution Control / Water Management / Aquatic Pollution","Soil Science & Conservation","Ecotoxicology"],"image":["https://media.springernature.com/lw1200/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1007%2Fs13762-026-07440-6/MediaObjects/13762_2026_7440_Figa_HTML.png","https://media.springernature.com/lw1200/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1007%2Fs13762-026-07440-6/MediaObjects/13762_2026_7440_Fig1_HTML.png","https://media.springernature.com/lw1200/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1007%2Fs13762-026-07440-6/MediaObjects/13762_2026_7440_Fig2_HTML.png","https://media.springernature.com/lw1200/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1007%2Fs13762-026-07440-6/MediaObjects/13762_2026_7440_Fig3_HTML.png","https://media.springernature.com/lw1200/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1007%2Fs13762-026-07440-6/MediaObjects/13762_2026_7440_Fig4_HTML.png","https://media.springernature.com/lw1200/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1007%2Fs13762-026-07440-6/MediaObjects/13762_2026_7440_Fig5_HTML.png","https://media.springernature.com/lw1200/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1007%2Fs13762-026-07440-6/MediaObjects/13762_2026_7440_Fig6_HTML.png","https://media.springernature.com/lw1200/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1007%2Fs13762-026-07440-6/MediaObjects/13762_2026_7440_Fig7_HTML.png"],"isPartOf":{"name":"International Journal of Environmental Science and Technology","issn":["1735-2630","1735-1472"],"volumeNumber":"23","@type":["Periodical","PublicationVolume"]},"publisher":{"name":"Springer Berlin Heidelberg","logo":{"url":"https://www.springernature.com/app-sn/public/images/logo-springernature.png","@type":"ImageObject"},"@type":"Organization"},"author":[{"name":"A. Taouda","affiliation":[{"name":"Sidi Mohamed Ben Abdellah University","address":{"name":"Laboratory of Materials, Processes, Catalysis, Agri-Food, and Environment, Higher School of Technology, Sidi Mohamed Ben Abdellah University, Fez, Morocco","@type":"PostalAddress"},"@type":"Organization"}],"@type":"Person"},{"name":"Y. Miyah","affiliation":[{"name":"Sidi Mohamed Ben Abdellah University","address":{"name":"Laboratory of Materials, Processes, Catalysis, Agri-Food, and Environment, Higher School of Technology, Sidi Mohamed Ben Abdellah University, Fez, Morocco","@type":"PostalAddress"},"@type":"Organization"},{"name":"Ministry of Health and Social Protection, Higher Institute of Nursing Professions and Health Techniques","address":{"name":"Ministry of Health and Social Protection, Higher Institute of Nursing Professions and Health Techniques, Fez, Morocco","@type":"PostalAddress"},"@type":"Organization"}],"@type":"Person"},{"name":"M. Benjelloun","url":"http://orcid.org/0000-0003-1086-3689","affiliation":[{"name":"Sidi Mohamed Ben Abdellah University","address":{"name":"Laboratory of Materials, Processes, Catalysis, Agri-Food, and Environment, Higher School of Technology, Sidi Mohamed Ben Abdellah University, Fez, Morocco","@type":"PostalAddress"},"@type":"Organization"}],"email":"mohammed.benjelloun4@usmba.ac.ma","@type":"Person"},{"name":"N. El Messaoudi","affiliation":[{"name":"Imam Mohammad Ibn Saud Islamic University (IMSIU)","address":{"name":"Department of Chemistry, College of Science, Imam Mohammad Ibn Saud Islamic University (IMSIU), Riyadh, Saudi Arabia","@type":"PostalAddress"},"@type":"Organization"}],"@type":"Person"},{"name":"S. Lairini","affiliation":[{"name":"Sidi Mohamed Ben Abdellah University","address":{"name":"Laboratory of Materials, Processes, Catalysis, Agri-Food, and Environment, Higher School of Technology, Sidi Mohamed Ben Abdellah University, Fez, Morocco","@type":"PostalAddress"},"@type":"Organization"}],"@type":"Person"}],"isAccessibleForFree":false,"hasPart":{"isAccessibleForFree":false,"cssSelector":".main-content","@type":"WebPageElement"},"@type":"ScholarlyArticle"},"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"WebPage"}</script>

        
        
